WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
<v Narrator>Edmondson defined team psychological safety as a shared belief held by members of a

2
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.750
team that the team is safe for interpersonal risk taking.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.700
Interpersonal risks are ordinary work acts that could make someone look ignorant,

4
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.050
incompetent or disruptive.

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.400
Asking a question.

6
00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.750
Admitting a mistake.

7
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.100
Raising a concern.

8
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.250
Disagreeing with someone more senior.

9
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.800
Notice two things about that definition.

10
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.550
First, it is shared.

11
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.500
It describes how a group works, not how confident one person is.

12
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:37.250
Second, it is about expectations: what people believe will happen if they take that

13
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.600
small risk.

14
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.750
So the question is not whether someone is naturally bold.

15
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:48.500
The question is how the team responds when someone takes an ordinary work risk.

16
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:54.050
<v Narrator>Her 1999 study looked at fifty-one work teams in a manufacturing company.

17
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:59.000
Psychological safety was associated with more learning behavior, such as asking for

18
00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.550
help, discussing errors and raising uncertainty.

19
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.700
Learning behavior, in turn, was associated with better team performance.

20
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.050
The readings are careful about what that means.

21
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.600
The study shows association, not proof that one causes the other.

22
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.950
The pattern has appeared in many settings since.

23
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:24.300
Still, it does not certify any team at DHS, and it is not a way to diagnose an

24
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:25.650
employee.

25
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.800
Think of it as a useful lens for looking at how your team handles uncertainty.

26
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.950
<v Narrator>Here is the most common misunderstanding.

27
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.500
Psychological safety is not being nice, avoiding conflict or lowering expectations.

28
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:43.250
Edmondson's goal is high safety combined with high expectations.

29
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:50.200
A team can hold a demanding standard and still make it safe to say that something is

30
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:51.550
not working.

31
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.700
In fact, candor is what lets high standards be met.

32
00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:01.050
Her book describes candid organizations, such as the film studio Pixar, as demanding

33
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.400
rather than comfortable.

34
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.150
In her account, candor is how quality gets made.

35
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.700
Discomfort is normal when people learn.

36
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:13.250
The real question is whether speaking up gets punished or humiliated.

37
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.600
Leaders, in her account, still sanction clear violations.

38
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.750
Safety and accountability sit together.

39
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.100
<v Narrator>A smooth meeting can feel like a good sign.

40
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.450
Nobody objects.

41
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.800
Everyone nods.

42
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:31.950
But the readings warn that a meeting with no questions may reflect clarity, a missed

43
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.500
opportunity, or worry about the response.

44
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.450
The record alone cannot tell you which.

45
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.400
Agreement is useful when it is supported.

46
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.950
It does not show how the team would handle a challenge.

47
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.100
The opposite is also true.

48
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.850
A hard conversation does not mean learning is impossible.

49
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.000
So do not use calm as proof, and do not use conflict as proof either.

50
00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.950
Look at what actually happens to a question when someone asks it.

51
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:08.300
<v Narrator>A team needs room to challenge a conclusion without the challenge being treated as

52
00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.650
misconduct.

53
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.800
Minnesota Management and Budget's Respectful Workplace policy, Policy 1432, draws

54
00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.150
the same line.

55
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.900
It separates respectful disagreement and normal professional supervision from

56
00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:23.050
disrespectful conduct, such as shouting, threats, bullying or demeaning treatment.

57
00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.400
That gives you two questions to keep apart.

58
00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.950
Is the claim supported by evidence?

59
00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.100
And was the exchange respectful?

60
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.050
A colleague can be wrong and respectful.

61
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.800
A colleague can be right and still speak to someone in a demeaning way.

62
00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:44.750
Each question gets its own answer, and neither one cancels the other.

63
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.900
<v Narrator>Sometimes a leader tries to build safety by promising too much.

64
00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:52.050
Every idea will be accepted.

65
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.400
Nothing said here will ever leave this room.

66
00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.550
Nobody will be held accountable.

67
00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.300
Those promises sound welcoming.

68
00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:04.250
The readings say they go beyond both the concept and anyone's authority.

69
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.000
Ideas still need evidence.

70
00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:11.350
Some concerns a supervisor has to route formally, such as an allegation of

71
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:12.700
discrimination.

72
00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:14.050
Accountability still applies.

73
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.200
A more honest invitation sounds like this: “Raise questions here.

74
00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:24.350
Here is what is still open, who decides, and what has to go through another

75
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.500
process.” That is less dramatic.

76
00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.450
It is also much easier to believe.

77
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.000
The readings add one more distinction.

78
00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:36.950
Cultural safety, a team's ability to raise concerns, and an organization's systems

79
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.300
for reporting and responding are related, but different.

80
00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:44.450
One survey or one session does not measure all three.

